{# How to read a table document with the table tools, from the overview to one cell. The
   order follows the tool texts: the overview gives the sheets and the column ids that the
   other tools take. #}
Reading a spreadsheet

A spreadsheet, a CSV file or a table in a document is a table document. Name it by the file_hash and the collection of a search result.

1. {{ tool('table_overview') }} lists the sheets and columns with their ids.
2. Read rows with {{ tool('table_page') }}. One call gives 50 rows. Sort and filter the rows in the call. Give row_start to go to a row of a large sheet, and columns to read the columns after the 60th.
3. {{ tool('table_column_values') }} lists values in one column and their counts.
4. To find a value in a sheet, call {{ tool('table_search_cells') }}. Read surrounding rows
with {{ tool('table_page') }} when the match needs context.
5. When {{ tool('table_page') }} cuts a long cell, read it whole with {{ tool('table_cell') }}. Give row with the returned row_id, and column with the returned column id.

Give a sheet, row and column when the reader needs to find a value again.

To count rows by a value, call {{ tool('table_column_values') }} on that column. Do not count
the rows of a page of text yourself. State the total rows, the filters and the column.
One values call gives up to 200 values. When the result has a continuation, call read_more
until no continuation remains. A partial value list gives no complete distribution.
The value counts add up to the row total only for the same sheet, the same rows and the
same filters. A blank cell has no value, so blank cells make the sum smaller than the
row total. State the number of blank cells when you compare the sum with the total.
For example, a sheet of 24 building permits has a type column. The values call gives
fence 9, roof 7, deck 5 and pool 3, and no continuation. These counts add up to 24. Counts
that add up to 27 for 24 rows show a counting error. Counts that add up to 21 show 3 rows
with a blank type.
A CSV file states no header row. Each table result gives `interpretation`, the reading of
row 1 that its counts describe. With state `established`, the reading is known. With state
`ambiguous` or `legacy` and two `candidates`, row 1 can be the header or the first data row.
Each candidate gives its row count. The result reads every row, `all_rows`, unless the
call gives `interpretation`. Give each count with its reading, or read the other reading
with interpretation `all_rows` or `first_row_header`. Do not report one reading's count as
the established row count.
A JSON file or a text file has no sheets. When {{ tool('table_overview') }} gives no sheet,
read the document as text.

Every table call requires collection and file_hash from the same document result.
{{ tool('table_overview') }} returns sheet and column identifiers.
The other table tools require sheet. Copy its identifier from the overview.
{{ tool('table_page') }} optionally accepts sort, filters, columns, search, row_start and interpretation.
sort requires column and direction, asc or desc. Omission uses the stored row order.
filters is a list of column predicates. Each predicate uses one test type.
A numeric or date range can include both lower and upper bounds.
Tests are contains, equals, starts_with, is_empty, number_min, number_max, date_min and date_max.
Text tests use strings. Number tests use numbers. Date bounds use YYYY-MM-DD strings.
columns selects column identifiers. One returned window contains at most 60 selected columns.
search optionally keeps rows containing its text. row_start is a zero-based position in the requested row order.
The result contains column metadata and rows. row_id is the stored row identity, including after sorting.

{{ tool('table_column_values') }} also requires column. search optionally filters value text.
It returns values and counts for that column.
{{ tool('table_search_cells') }} requires query. It returns matching sheet, row and column identities with text.
{{ tool('table_cell') }} requires row and column. Pass the returned row_id under the argument name row.
It returns 2,000-character slices. Use read_more for the remaining cell text.
Use read_more for continued table pages, values and search hits.

This minimal overview opens the table structure.
```json
{"collection":"alpha","file_hash":"COPY_RETURNED_HASH"}
```
This page call uses copied sheet and column identifiers.
```json
{"collection":"alpha","file_hash":"COPY_RETURNED_HASH","sheet":0,"sort":{"column":2,"direction":"asc"},"filters":[{"column":2,"contains":"training"}],"columns":[0,2,5],"search":"active","row_start":50}
```
This values call narrows one column.
```json
{"collection":"alpha","file_hash":"COPY_RETURNED_HASH","sheet":0,"column":2,"search":"training"}
```
This values call reads row 1 of an ambiguous CSV sheet as the column names.
```json
{"collection":"alpha","file_hash":"COPY_RETURNED_HASH","sheet":0,"column":2,"interpretation":"first_row_header"}
```
This cell search locates a requested value.
```json
{"collection":"alpha","file_hash":"COPY_RETURNED_HASH","sheet":0,"query":"Greenvelope"}
```
This cell call reads row_id 71, even when that row appeared first in a sorted result.
```json
{"collection":"alpha","file_hash":"COPY_RETURNED_HASH","sheet":0,"row":71,"column":2}
```
